Windows amd64 AppContainer runner

This directory is a standalone Go module. Build with Go >=1.26 (required by
pinned golang.org/x/sys v0.48.0). The executable needs only Windows system DLLs;
it does not need Go, PowerShell, a globally installed Node, or administrator rights
at runtime. Windows 10/11 amd64 are the intended acceptance targets.

Protocol

  windows-runner.exe --root <absolute-private-staging-directory> \
    --parent <calling-Node-PID> --timeout-ms <1..22000> -- <absolute.exe> [args...]

The JS caller creates a NEW private directory for each invocation, copies the
Node+SDK or FFmpeg runtime and all inputs into it, and removes it after the runner
exits. Never pass an existing plugin directory, HOME, or another shared directory.
The runner rejects drive roots, HOME and its ancestors, Windows directory trees,
UNC/device paths, reparse points (including ancestors), hard-linked staged files,
and executables outside root. Use a local NTFS directory supporting Windows ACLs.
Other processes owned by the same user must not concurrently mutate this staging
tree. This launcher isolates the payload, not other code running as that user.

The child receives stdin and stdout directly through an explicit three-handle
inheritance allowlist. Its stderr goes to NUL. Stdin EOF completes the input; it
DOES NOT cancel execution. Arguments must contain only staged paths and fixed
code/parameters, never account credentials. Read stdout while the child runs to
avoid filling its pipe. The child's working directory is root.

The child environment contains only:

  APPDATA, LOCALAPPDATA, TEMP, TMP, USERPROFILE = root
  PATH = Windows System32
  SystemRoot = Windows installation directory

No NODE_OPTIONS, account environment, user PATH, or DLL search paths are copied.

Cancellation and statuses

The caller cancels by creating root/.cancel. The runner checks every <=50 ms
while waiting for the child, closes the Job, waits for termination, restores
security descriptors, and deletes the AppContainer profile. The JS caller should
wait for this cleanup; it can kill the launcher only as a last-resort fallback.
The timeout starts before sandbox setup, and the remaining budget is checked
before the suspended child is resumed. Preparation/cleanup perform synchronous
Windows calls and are not guaranteed to finish inside that execution budget.

Exit status:

  child status  child finished normally (forwarded without translation)
  124           launcher deadline exceeded
  125           bad request, parent exited, isolation/setup/wait/cleanup failure
  130           root/.cancel requested cancellation

A child can itself return these same values. Launcher diagnostics on stderr are
fixed strings without arguments, paths, usernames, OS errors, or child stderr.

Isolation and lifecycle

Each run creates a cryptographically unique AppContainer profile with ZERO
capabilities, including no internet/private-network capabilities. There is no
ordinary-process fallback. Windows system resources exposed to AppContainers
remain accessible; the intended boundary is private user data and write access,
not complete invisibility of every filesystem object outside root.

Only the staged tree receives the profile SID: read/write/execute/delete access,
without WRITE_DAC or WRITE_OWNER. Its DACLs are temporarily protected and limited
to the launching user, SYSTEM, and that profile SID. Low mandatory-integrity
labels allow the low-integrity child to create outputs. Existing DACLs and labels
are restored after the child terminates; originally implicit medium labels may
be restored as explicit medium labels. New output objects lose the per-run SID
and receive a private user/SYSTEM DACL and medium label. Cleanup does not delete
payload files; deleting the task's staging directory is the caller's job.

CreateProcess uses SECURITY_CAPABILITIES and CREATE_SUSPENDED. Windows 10+ atomic
PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_JOB_LIST puts the child into a guard Job during creation,
closing the launcher-crash window before explicit assignment. The runner then
calls AssignProcessToJobObject to place the still-suspended child into a nested
execution Job, and resumes its thread. BOTH Jobs use KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE and
ActiveProcessLimit=1; neither permits breakaway. The child cannot create another
process. Unsupported atomic membership fails closed, without fallback.

A held parent-process handle avoids PID reuse during monitoring. Parent exit,
timeout, or cancellation close both Jobs and wait for the child before ACL/profile
cleanup. Job and parent handles are never inherited. Abnormal launcher termination
also closes the last Job handles, so Windows kills the child even if the launcher
was killed before the explicit Assign call. A forcibly terminated launcher cannot
execute ACL/profile cleanup, so its private staging tree and unique profile may
remain; prefer .cancel and allow normal cleanup.

Build and checks

The runtime ZIP retains the runner source and module locks. Test sources and
prebuilt integration tests are included only in the separate -validation.zip
(or the source repository). Use either of those when running the test commands.

From this directory on macOS/Linux, use fresh output paths:

  out=$(mktemp -d /tmp/qishui-windows-runner.XXXXXX)
  GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o "$out/windows-runner.exe" .
  GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 CGO_ENABLED=0 go test -c -o "$out/integration.test.exe" ./tests
  GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 CGO_ENABLED=0 go vet ./...

These are cross-compilation/static checks, NOT a Windows sandbox execution test.

On Windows amd64, with a freshly created output directory (cmd.exe syntax):

  go build -o "%RUNNER_TEST_OUT%\windows-runner.exe" .
  go test -c -o "%RUNNER_TEST_OUT%\integration.test.exe" ./tests
  set QISHUI_WINDOWS_RUNNER=%RUNNER_TEST_OUT%\windows-runner.exe
  set QISHUI_TEST_WINDOWS=1
  "%RUNNER_TEST_OUT%\integration.test.exe" -test.v -test.timeout=120s

Set RUNNER_TEST_OUT to an unused directory before these commands. Alternatively,
set the same two QISHUI variables and run go test -v -timeout 120s ./tests.
Without QISHUI_TEST_WINDOWS=1, real Windows integration tests skip.

The tests stage a copy of the SAME Go test exe as a payload, then check:

- AppContainer token, controlled environment, staged read and nested output write.
- Read/overwrite/create denial against an external private user/SYSTEM fixture.
- TCP denial against a real local listener with an unsandboxed positive control.
- Additional child process refusal, EOF response completion, and SID ACL cleanup.
- Deadline, monitored-parent exit, and .cancel killing a confirmed child PID.
- Missing/relative/file root, missing/outside executable, invalid timeout, hardlinks.

They do not execute Node/SDK/FFmpeg, test public internet routes, force-kill the
launcher, or assert deletion of the profile via registry inspection. Full Windows
packaging/runtime and forced-termination acceptance remain integration work.
No Windows execution result is claimed from a macOS build.
